INT. REHEARSAL STUDIO - FORMER BELL FOUNDRY - AFTERNOON

A long brick hall that smells of cold metal and dust. Sunlight slants through high windows onto a row of bronze bells hung on a rack like sleeping bats. A single upright piano, lid open.

OSWALDO "OZZIE" TREMBLAY (60s, choirmaster, silk scarf, a frail dignity) stands at the piano, turning pages of a score that does not need turning.

Three chairs. Only two are filled.

DELPHINE MARCHAND (30s, soprano, beautiful in a distracted way) hums a scale under her breath.

HIRO TANABE (30s, baritone, sleeves rolled, forearm tattoo of a tide chart) studies his phone.

The third chair is empty. Ozzie glances at it. Glances away.

OZZIE
Shall we start from the Agnus Dei?

DELPHINE
Ozzie, Margit isn't here.

OZZIE
She's running late.

HIRO
She's been running late for six weeks.

OZZIE
Hiro.

HIRO
I'm only saying.

DELPHINE
She texted me. She said she'd be here.

OZZIE
There. You see?

He sets the score on the piano. Presses a chord. It rings in the hall for a long moment and slowly dies against the bronze.

DELPHINE
It's nice. The light. It's nice in here today.

OZZIE
It is.

He smiles at her. It is a kind smile and it takes nearly everything he has.

The telephone at the back of the hall rings. An old black rotary. Everyone looks. Nobody rises. It rings six times. Ozzie crosses at last and lifts the receiver.

OZZIE (CONT'D)
Foundry. Yes.

He listens. His hand tightens on the cord.

MARGIT (V.O.)
(through the receiver, thin)
Ozzie. It's me. I'm at the hospital.

OZZIE
Where, Margit?

MARGIT (V.O.)
St. Anne's. They said it's... They said it's quiet. They said you'd want to know before the others.

OZZIE
(softly)
Thank you, my dear.

He hangs up. For a moment he doesn't turn. Then he does, and the two of them look at his face, and they know.

HIRO
Ozzie?

DELPHINE
Who was it?

OZZIE
The thing about a bell is that it doesn't ring because you strike it.

HIRO
Ozzie.

OZZIE
It rings because of what it's shaped like on the inside.

He takes a mallet from the rack. Lifts it. The largest bell swings gently, and he strikes it once. The note fills the hall, enormous and golden, and the two singers stand slowly as it fades.

FADE OUT.
